//? Title: Receptive Fields: Enclosing Boxes and Actual Derivatives
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A local CNN feature depends on only part of an image, but its architectural receptive box
and its active input derivatives are different objects. We track size, jump and centre through
two convolution/pooling blocks and verify the predicted support with positive linear filters.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.1_fundamentals/receptive_fields.cpp`.

> The method

Starting with receptive width r=1, input jump j=1 and first pixel centre a=.5, one layer gives

$$
r'=r+(d(k-1))j,\quad j'=sj,\quad a'=a+\left(\frac{d(k-1)}2-p_{before}\right)j.
$$
\alttext(Receptive width grows by effective-kernel minus one times the old jump; stride multiplies the jump and padding shifts the first centre.)

For SAME 3×3 convolution, 2×2 VALID pooling, then the same pair, widths are 3,4,8,10;
jumps are 1,2,2,4 and first centres .5,1,1,2. Output index u has centre a+uj. Pixels are
centred at integer+.5, so a width-ten interior unit centred at 14 covers indices 9 through 18.
The box clips at image boundaries. All-positive filters and average pooling activate every
reachable path; ReLU, signed cancellation and max-pooling may reduce a trained unit's support.
Geometry tracking is O(layers); an input derivative costs one backward evaluation.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/receptive_fields_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/receptive_fields_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/receptive_fields_concept.tikz > build/receptive_fields_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/receptive_fields_concept.svg build/receptive_fields_concept.dvi > build/receptive_fields_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
