> Checking the theory

A finite difference probes an inside pixel, while a far-away perturbation must leave the selected feature unchanged.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  auto x = n::ones({1, 28, 28, 1}, type).requires_grad_();
  auto feature = [](const n::Tensor &input) {
    auto h = input;
    for (int stage = 0; stage < 2; ++stage) {
      h = n::conv2d(h, n::ones({3, 3, 1, 1}, input.dtype()));
      h = n::avg_pool2d(h, 2, 2);
    }
    return n::sum(n::slice(h, {0, 3, 3, 0}, {1, 1, 1, 1}));
  };
  const auto gradient = n::grad(feature(x), {x})[0];
  auto values = x.to_vector<double>();
  const double eps = 1e-5;
  values[14 * 28 + 14] += eps;
  const double plus = feature(n::from_vector(values, x.shape())).item();
  values[14 * 28 + 14] -= 2 * eps;
  const double minus = feature(n::from_vector(values, x.shape())).item();
  const double numeric = (plus - minus) / (2 * eps), analytic = gradient.at({0, 14, 14, 0});
  values = x.to_vector<double>();
  values[27 * 28 + 27] += 100;
  const double outside =
      std::abs(feature(n::from_vector(values, x.shape())).item() - feature(x).item());
  if (std::abs(numeric - analytic) > 1e-8 || outside != 0)
    return 1;
  std::print("Inside derivative: autograd {:.9f}, finite difference {:.9f}; outside "
             "perturbation effect {}.\n",
             analytic, numeric, outside);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Inside derivative: autograd 2.250000000, finite difference 2.250000000; outside perturbation effect 0.
// result_end


> Conclusion

The size/jump/centre recursion predicts the full positive-path support, including boundary
clipping. Finite differences confirm one active derivative and one unreachable input. A trained
network's local derivative may occupy less of that architectural box.

> References

- [Dumoulin and Visin (2016), A guide to convolution arithmetic for deep learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.07285).
